   $q$A sequence is given by $a_n = 7 \cdot 4^{n}$ for $n \geq 0$, so its first term is $a_0 = 7$. The same list of terms is to be written with the first term numbered $n = 1$. Which formula, for $n \geq 1$, gives the same list?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$b_n = 4 \\cdot 7^{n-1}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"swapped-first-term-and-ratio"},{"text":"$b_n = 7 \\cdot 4^{n}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"kept-exponent-after-shifting-start"},{"text":"$b_n = 7 \\cdot 4^{n-1}$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$b_n = 7 \\cdot 4^{n+1}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"shifted-index-in-wrong-direction"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$b_n = 7 \cdot 4^{n-1}$$q$, $q$The list is $7, 28, 112, \dots$. With the first term at $n = 1$, the exponent must be $0$ when $n = 1$, so use $n - 1$: $b_n = 7 \cdot 4^{n-1}$, which gives $b_1 = 7$ and $b_2 = 28$. The formula $7 \cdot 4^{n}$ gives $28$ when $n = 1$, so its list starts one term late.$q$, 'draft', null)
